module gunbc.whole_corpus_compile_admission

import std.types { Int, String, NonEmptyStr, Bool }
import std.measure { ByteSize, byte_size, byte_size_count }
import std.disposition { Disposition, Terminal, Scaffold, SingleAuthority }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.roadmap_model { RoadmapNodeId }
import gunbc.seed_growth { SeedGrowthJustification }
import gunbc.host_budget_source {
  HostBudgetResolution,
  HostBudgetResolved,
  HostBudgetUnreadable,
  HostBudgetSource,
  host_budget_source_label,
}

data whole_corpus_compile_admission_authority_disposition: Disposition = Terminal {
  reason: "Single authority for the arm-time admission of a WHOLE-CORPUS compile against the host memory budget gunbc.host_budget_source already models. The budget half and the source half were both modeled; the JOIN between them and the decision to start the run was not, which is the defect this module closes. Its sibling for the floor walk is gunbc.runner_slot_allocation gunbc_floor_minimum_viable_armed_budget, enforced by claim_executor at arm time as a fail-fast refusal on a provably doomed walk; nothing played that role for the compile instrument, and this module is that role, derived the same way from a measured demand figure."
}

data whole_corpus_compile_unjoined_observation_note: String = "THE OBSERVATION EXISTED, WAS CORRECT, AND WAS JOINED TO NOTHING — which is why the fix is a join and not a bigger machine. v1_compiler.cli_run typed_module_cache_cap derives an entry cap from the host budget once per index and, when the budget source is degraded, prints [floor-drain] degraded_budget_source: cap=<n> source=<label>. That line is a write-only eprintln: no consumer reads it, no admission decision reads the budget it was derived from, and the process then starts a whole-corpus resolve whose resident set exceeds what that budget can hold.\n\nA run that knows its budget and starts a job it cannot hold is not resource-starved, it is UN-JOINED. Resource-starved is answered by a larger machine; un-joined produces the identical failure on the larger machine one corpus-growth later. DESIGN section 5 names the failure shape exactly: the deficit's frequency is zero by construction, so it never ranks for fixing, and the cost arrives as a dead process instead of a diagnostic.\n\nHOW IT ARRIVES, and why it is worse than a crash: the kill is SIGKILL. There is no diagnostic, no partial-result marker, and a harness that greps the captured output for a count reads ZERO — a fabricated zero indistinguishable from a real one. The session that found this had a harness printing only the grep result and would have published 'the corpus carries 0 rows of class X'; echoing the exit status explicitly is the only thing that stopped it. Every corpus-wide count taken over a run whose exit status was not recorded is unattributed for the same reason, and that is a standing reading rule rather than a fact about one night."

data whole_corpus_compile_observed_kill_receipt_note: String = "MEASURED, TWICE, ON THE BUILDBUDDY REMOTE-EXECUTION RUNNER (session stern-heron-695, 2026-08-19/20). The instrument is the one v1.compiler.04_infer method_existence_wall_note names as the whole-corpus instrument — gunbc compile --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --target dag — and which that row contrasts against a narrow --entry run that reported clean while twelve real sites sat outside its closure. Invocations a39713da-8cfb-415d-a8f6-1e0ef150d075 (at 517fdac4dd) and 13cf8d2e-173a-42d2-9a56-101bb3332740 (at 0456098671) both ended 'Killed' with EXIT=137. The BUILD succeeded in about four minutes each time; the COMPILE died. Reproducible, not a flake.\n\nTHE CAP LINE IS THE PREDICTION, AND ITS UNITS ARE ENTRIES, NOT BYTES. The second run printed, immediately before dying: [floor-drain] degraded_budget_source: cap=1675 source=/proc/meminfo MemAvailable (no private cgroup memory.max/memory.high found). 1675 is a MODULE-ENTRY count, not a megabyte figure — the reporting session read it as '1675 MB' and derived from that a '~1.6 GiB runner', which is corrected here rather than inherited. cli_run derives cap as budget_bytes / TYPED_MODULE_BYTES_PER_ENTRY_ESTIMATE (3 MiB) clamped to [100, 4000], and 1675 sits strictly inside that clamp, so the reading INVERTS EXACTLY: MemAvailable on that runner was in [5269094400, 5272240128) bytes — about 4.9 GiB, not 1.6. THE CAPACITY INVERSION SURVIVES THE CORRECTION and is the operator-facing half: the session container carries a 31 GiB cgroup cap while the remote-execution runner answered with under 5 GiB, so the smaller machine is the one that ran it. WHAT IS MEASURED AND WHAT IS NOT, separated 2026-08-28 because this clause asserted a cause it never established. MEASURED: both runs occurred on BuildBuddy, the runner exposed about 4.9 GiB, both ended by SIGKILL. NOT ESTABLISHED: why they went there. The clause read 'remote is the DEFAULT route, so a memory-bound job is sent to the small machine automatically' — that default is a fact about the cargo shim, which does not route a bare `gunbc compile` binary, and no invocation record here says how these two were dispatched. The causal story is WITHDRAWN rather than repaired, because the receipts ground the resource failure without it.\n\nWHY THE CORRECTION MAKES THE JOIN MORE URGENT RATHER THAN LESS. Against the measured demand below the deficit is of order one to two gibibytes, not a factor of four — and a deficit that small is exactly the one a bigger machine appears to fix. An un-joined instrument that gets a bigger machine survives to fail again, silently, on the next corpus growth. That is the whole argument for landing a refusal rather than a requisition."

data whole_corpus_compile_measured_demand_note: String = "WHERE THE THRESHOLD COMES FROM, AND WHY IT IS NOT A NUMBER FROM THIS TREE (DESIGN section 5 test-oracle rule: a merge-blocking literal must be grounded in a controlled fixture, an external or versioned authority, or a policy budget — and a measurement copied from the current tree is not an oracle).\n\nTHE PROXY IS RETIRED. This figure was 7 GiB, derived from two 2026-07-21 CI receipts that measured the compile-clean whole-tree pass on the FLOOR route rather than gunbc compile's own emit leg, and that note declared its own re-measure trigger: a dated uncensored whole-tree peak taken on the compile route itself. THAT TRIGGER HAS FIRED and this row is rewritten rather than amended, because every load-bearing sentence of the proxy version was a statement about a substitute that is no longer needed.\n\nTHE RECEIPT. Session clever-tern-899, 2026-08-28, on srv1 (502 GiB total, 334 GiB available at arm time, aarch64), against a clean tree staged at 91c05c1b344d29f97a363eaff34843177d552a99 with a binary BUILT FROM THAT SAME SHA. Both targets were measured, since the admission arm does not discriminate on target:\n\n  gunbc compile --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --target dag   EXIT=1  peak 13008052 kB  wall 25:04\n  gunbc compile --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --target rust  EXIT=1  peak 13005964 kB  wall 27:44\n\nEXIT STATUS AND PEAK WERE CAPTURED TOGETHER, per whole_corpus_compile_unjoined_observation_note: a SIGKILL prints nothing and reads as a clean zero, so a peak without an exit status is unattributed. Both runs COMPLETED and refused on diagnostics; neither was killed. A POSITIVE CONTROL ran first — the scoped compile of src/v2/std/node.dag returned EXIT=0 with a file emitted — so the harness produces both outcomes and a refusal is a finding rather than a broken rig.\n\nUNCENSORED IN THE SENSE gunbc.runner_slot_allocation gunbc_floor_measured_peak_is_uncensored DEMANDS: the host had 271 GiB available at exit against a 12.4 GiB peak, so nothing throttled either run and neither peak is an armed line being mistaken for demand.\n\nTHE GRAIN IS THE RECEIPT'S GRAIN, ROUNDED IN THE FAIL-CLOSED DIRECTION. The higher reading is 13008052 kB = 12.41 GiB; the declared figure is that rounded UP to whole-gibibyte grain — 13 GiB — because for a DEMAND figure rounding up refuses the marginal case and rounding down admits it. The two targets agree to within 0.02%, which is why one figure serves both rather than each target carrying its own.\n\nWHAT THIS CORRECTS, STATED AS A FAIL-OPEN RATHER THAN AS STALENESS. 7 GiB was declared a LOWER bound and was 43.6% BELOW the measured peak — 7 GiB against 12.41 GiB, or 56.4% of it — so the arm ADMITTED hosts it would then be killed on. (This read '64% under' until 2026-08-28. That figure was computed as (11.5-7)/7 against the CONFOUNDED 11.5 GiB peak this change retracts, expressed as a percentage ABOVE the old declaration, then carried across and relabelled 'under' when the peak was corrected to 12.41 — wrong denominator and a withdrawn measurement, in one number. The percentage now names its denominator, which is the only form a reader can check.) A refusal that cannot fire for the population needing it is not conservative; it is the shape DESIGN section 5 names, where the deficit's frequency is zero by construction. The 15 GiB CI runner slot still admits, but the headroom is now about 21% rather than the better-than-2x the old figure implied.\n\nEVERY PEAK ABOVE IS A REFUSING RUN'S PEAK, AND THAT IS A BOUND RATHER THAN THE DEMAND. Both runs exited 1 on diagnostics, so neither paid for the stages after the gate it stopped at. A third run was taken to close that gap and DID NOT CLOSE IT (session clever-tern-899, srv1, the same binary and staged tree, --target rust with the three parameter-default sites patched to literal defaults): EXIT=1, peak 12986524 kB, wall 25:05, zero files emitted. Clearing the parameter-default gate revealed EIGHT anonymous-record refusals standing behind it -- ambiguous anonymous record literal shapes in dag/test/claim/access_validation_test.dag (5), dag/extdeps/git/versioning.dag, dag/extdeps/realization/reconcile_in_process.dag and dag/test/claim/cache_retention_axes_witness_test.dag -- reproducing on the live corpus exactly the three-then-eight prefix that gunbc.emit_stage_blocking_population_census already records. So the rust route's blocking population is still not bounded, and no completing whole-corpus compile has been measured by this session.\n\nWHAT THE THIRD RUN DID ESTABLISH, because a failed attempt at one question can answer another: the peak did not move. 12986524 kB against 13005964 kB is 0.15% LOWER while stopping at a strictly later gate, so the resident set is dominated by the shared front half -- frontend, normalize, reconcile -- and not by emission. A refusing peak is therefore a STABLE floor across gates rather than a figure that creeps toward the true demand as gates clear, which means the remaining gap to a completing run cannot be estimated from these three readings at all.\n\nAN ATTRIBUTED OBSERVATION THIS SESSION DID NOT REPRODUCE, recorded because omitting it would leave the reader with three agreeing numbers and no reason to doubt them: warm-ant-908 reports a COMPLETING --target dag whole-corpus run (exit 0, zero blocking, wall 27:52) peaking at 15871708 kB = 15.14 GiB, on srv1, under a patched tree at be89e236c9. That is 2.7 GiB above every figure in this row. It is NOT adopted here and is NOT the declared threshold: this session did not take it, the tree it was taken on is not the tree this row measures, and a demand figure carried on someone else's receipt is the transcription this document forbids. It is recorded as the reason the threshold below is known to be a lower bound rather than merely stated to be one. Read against gunbc.runner_slot_allocation's two lines, 15.14 GiB sits ABOVE the 15 GiB memory_high throttle and BELOW the 16 GiB memory_max kill line, so a completing run in a runner slot degrades into swap rather than dying -- the slot hosts it, slower.\n\nRE-MEASURE TRIGGER, NARROWED BECAUSE THE PROXY IS GONE: a dated uncensored whole-corpus peak on the compile route, taken with a binary built from the subject sha, that EXCEEDS this figure. A demand figure does not shrink with corpus growth, so a lower later reading is not a reason to lower this one. THE VINTAGE CLAUSE IS PART OF THE TRIGGER AND NOT DECORATION: the first attempt at this measurement used a binary 91 stage0 Rust files behind its subject and reported a DIFFERENT BLOCKING POPULATION — one diagnostic of an entirely different class — so a peak taken under a mismatched compiler measures neither the tree nor the compiler anyone runs. Deliberately NOT re-derived by scaling this peak by a module count; the relationship between corpus size and peak resident set is still unmeasured, and a fabricated scaling law would be a heuristic standing where a measurement was available (DESIGN section 4)."

data whole_corpus_compile_rung_note: String = "RUNG: MITIGATABLE, and saying so is load-bearing because the neighbouring claim would be inflation. What this admission does is convert a SILENT SIGKILL — which sits below the ladder entirely, being a fabricated zero rather than any rung of it — into a typed, located, exit-1 refusal that names the two disagreeing quantities, the budget, its source, and a remedy. That is containment of harm at a boundary, not prevention of the state.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM: an admitted budget is NOT certified sufficient. The threshold is now a peak taken on the compile route itself, at a named sha, with a binary built from it — so the 'different route at an older tree' qualification this clause used to carry is retired. What survives is narrower and still true: it is ONE tree's peak, and a demand figure does not shrink with corpus growth, so admission means 'not provably doomed at the tree that was measured', never 'will fit'. A run that clears this and dies anyway is a re-measure trigger, not a defect in the refusal.\n\nNEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: a per-run resident-set prediction derived from the compile's own declared inputs rather than a fleet-wide constant, at which point the admission becomes a real fit predicate and can climb toward mechanical prevention. Until then the class is declared here rather than left to be rediscovered."

data whole_corpus_compile_scope_note: String = "SCOPED COMPILES REACH NO ADMISSION ARM AT ALL, and that placement is unchanged — but THE ASYMMETRY THIS NOTE USED TO REST ON HAS CLOSED, and saying so is the point of this paragraph. It read that a scoped compile 'was measured to fit on the very runner that killed the whole-corpus run'. Re-measured 2026-08-28 (session clever-tern-899, srv1, same tree and binary as whole_corpus_compile_measured_demand_note): the scoped compile of src/v2/std/node.dag returns EXIT=0 with one file emitted and peaks at 5768388 kB — about 5.5 GiB, ABOVE the roughly 4.9 GiB that runner reported. So it no longer fits there either, and a reader taking the old sentence would be reassured about a fit that does not exist.\n\nWHAT SURVIVES THE CORRECTION, because the note's actual argument never depended on that fit: a scoped compile's resident working set is one module's reference-derived closure and not the whole tree, so a whole-tree demand figure is the wrong DENOMINATOR for it. That is a statement about grain, not about any particular runner, and it is why applying this threshold to scoped runs would refuse runs that demonstrably succeed. The gap between 5.5 GiB scoped and 12.4 GiB whole-corpus is the measured size of that grain difference.\n\nAn unasked question, not an all-clear: a scoped compile is not admitted by this module, it is outside its subject. If scoped compiles later need their own bound it will be a different measurement with a different demand figure, never this one reused at a discount."

// The demand figure: the higher of the two uncensored 2026-08-28 whole-corpus peaks taken ON THE
// COMPILE ROUTE ITSELF (--target dag 13008052 kB, --target rust 13005964 kB), rounded up to
// whole-gibibyte grain. See whole_corpus_compile_measured_demand_note for the grain argument, the
// instrument that re-derives it, and the re-measure trigger.
fn whole_corpus_compile_measured_peak_demand() -> ByteSize {
  byte_size(13958643712)
}

type WholeCorpusCompileAdmission
  = WholeCorpusCompileAdmitted { budget: ByteSize, required: ByteSize }
  | WholeCorpusCompileRefusedBudgetBelowMeasuredDemand {
      budget: ByteSize,
      required: ByteSize,
      source: NonEmptyStr,
    }
  | WholeCorpusCompileRefusedBudgetUnreadable { reason: NonEmptyStr }

fn whole_corpus_compile_admission_at_budget(
  bytes: ByteSize,
  source: HostBudgetSource
) -> WholeCorpusCompileAdmission {
  if byte_size_count(b: bytes) < byte_size_count(b: whole_corpus_compile_measured_peak_demand()) {
    WholeCorpusCompileRefusedBudgetBelowMeasuredDemand {
      budget: bytes,
      required: whole_corpus_compile_measured_peak_demand(),
      source: host_budget_source_label(s: source),
    }
  } else {
    WholeCorpusCompileAdmitted {
      budget: bytes,
      required: whole_corpus_compile_measured_peak_demand(),
    }
  }
}

fn whole_corpus_compile_admission(budget: HostBudgetResolution) -> WholeCorpusCompileAdmission {
  match budget {
    HostBudgetUnreadable { reason: r } => WholeCorpusCompileRefusedBudgetUnreadable { reason: r }
    HostBudgetResolved { source: s, bytes: b } =>
      whole_corpus_compile_admission_at_budget(bytes: b, source: s)
  }
}

fn whole_corpus_compile_admits(a: WholeCorpusCompileAdmission) -> Bool {
  match a {
    WholeCorpusCompileAdmitted { budget: _, required: _ } => true
    WholeCorpusCompileRefusedBudgetBelowMeasuredDemand { budget: _, required: _, source: _ } => false
    WholeCorpusCompileRefusedBudgetUnreadable { reason: _ } => false
  }
}

// The budget the BuildBuddy runner actually answered with, recovered exactly from the
// cap=1675 line that run printed (see whole_corpus_compile_observed_kill_receipt_note): the
// low end of the [5269094400, 5272240128) interval that division admits. Every value in the
// interval produces the same cap and the same verdict here.
data whole_corpus_compile_observed_runner_budget_lower_bound: ByteSize = byte_size(5269094400)

data whole_corpus_compile_seed_mirror_disposition: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.whole_corpus_compile_admission",
    decl_name: "whole_corpus_compile_admission",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

data whole_corpus_compile_seed_mirror_note: String = "v1 HAND-REALIZES THIS, FOR THE SAME BOOTSTRAP REASON gunbc.host_budget_source host_budget_source_seed_mirror_note and gunbc.typed_module_cache_capacity typed_module_cache_capacity_seed_mirror_note give, and no other: the decision runs BEFORE any .dag value could exist, because it is the decision about whether resolving the corpus may begin at all. It cannot be a value obtained by resolving the corpus. That is a bootstrap constraint, not ordinary seed-shrink debt, and conflating the two would misprice the work. The mirror is v1_compiler.memory_governor DECLARED_WHOLE_CORPUS_COMPILE_MEASURED_DEMAND_BYTES and whole_corpus_compile_admission over the same budget authority read_host_budget_bytes, joined in v1_compiler.cli_run `compile_emission`, on that transaction's PrimaryRoot arm, before the module index is built and deliberately not asked of Entry.\n\nTHIS SENTENCE NAMED main.rs UNTIL 2026-08-28 AND THE JOIN IS NOT THERE, which is worth recording because of what the stale citation cost a reader. A reviewer searching main.rs for the call site found none, and concluded from its absence that NO PRODUCTION CALLER EXISTS — that the arm is dormant and the refusal has never executed. That inference was wrong and the citation is why it was available. Refuted by EXECUTION on 2026-08-28: a whole-root compile under GUNBC_MEMORY_BUDGET_BYTES=5368709120 exits 1 with WholeCorpusCompileBudgetBelowMeasuredDemand naming both quantities, the source and the remedy, while a scoped --entry compile under the SAME budget is not refused by this arm and proceeds to the frontend. Two arms, one varied input, opposite outcomes: the arm is wired and the Entry asymmetry is real. A grep of the file a note names is not a census of callers, and the remedy for that is to name the symbol that holds the call rather than the file someone remembers it in (DESIGN section 3).\n\nTHIS ROW DECLARES NO DISSOLUTION OF ITS OWN, AND THAT IS THE POINT. typed_module_cache_capacity_seed_mirror_note already specifies the exact mechanism that retires this constant: one lens reading the seed's constants against the rows that own them, which it says would cover gunbc.typed_module_cache_capacity, gunbc.host_budget_source and DECLARED_RUNNER_SLOT_MEMORY_HIGH_BYTES together. DECLARED_WHOLE_CORPUS_COMPILE_MEASURED_DEMAND_BYTES lives in the same file as that last one and is retired by the same lens on the same day. Authoring a fourth locally-argued dissolution beside three identical ones is the forked-logic tell DESIGN section 3 names — the merge is already specified, so this row joins it rather than restating it. The wider dissolution is the same one those notes carry: the emit path bakes these constants into the seed and the seed stops composing its own arithmetic.\n\nTHE TRIGGER WAS INERT WHEN THIS ROW WAS WRITTEN AND IS BEING BUILT NOW; a reader must not infer a guard from the citation above without reading this paragraph. Measured at 517fdac4dd by stern-heron-695 and re-checked here: dag/test/claim/typed_module_cache_capacity_witness_test.dag carried eight test fns and referenced no .rs path, no stage0 projection, and not the per-entry byte literal — it checked the model against itself. memory_governor.rs is a hand_maintained_stage0_filenames() member and so is excluded from the derived generated population (gunbc.stage0_rust_source_lifecycle_scaffold derived_generated_stage0_repo_paths), confirming it is hand-maintained source rather than a generated projection, so this constant is knowingly-added seed source. The cost of the gap was not hypothetical: DECLARED_RUNNER_SLOT_MEMORY_HIGH_BYTES sat eleven days at a value its authority had superseded, under a note that read as though the drift were guarded.\n\nWHAT IS LANDING, AND WHAT IT WILL AND WILL NOT BUY. gunbc#8638 builds that lens (test.claim.seed_mirror_constant_lens_witness_test), joining seed constants to their authority rows and REFUSING an uncited or unparseable one rather than skipping it. Its in-scope set is an ENUMERATED list, not a set derived from the seed file, so a constant that is not on it — including this one at the time of writing — is neither checked nor refused; joining is a row someone adds, which is why the seed constant here is written as a canonical decimal literal and names its authority symbol in a doc comment, so that adding the row is a one-liner. Its ENROLLMENT MARKER is deliberately withheld: that lens's backward arm requires marker occurrences per seed file to equal roster rows homed in that file, so a marked constant with no row reds main for however long the two land apart. Row and marker land together, in whichever PR is second. When it lands the class becomes MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE and not structural: the invalid state stays writable and safety depends on the lens executing and staying enrolled. THIS PARAGRAPH IS REWRITTEN, NOT AMENDED, WHEN THE SIXTH ROW LANDS."


data whole_corpus_compile_seed_growth_justification: SeedGrowthJustification = SeedGrowthJustification {
  hand_authored_declarations: [
    DeclarationRef {
      module_path: "v1_compiler.memory_governor",
      decl_name: "DECLARED_WHOLE_CORPUS_COMPILE_MEASURED_DEMAND_BYTES",
      field: WholeDeclaration
    },
    DeclarationRef {
      module_path: "v1_compiler.memory_governor",
      decl_name: "WholeCorpusCompileAdmission",
      field: WholeDeclaration
    },
    DeclarationRef {
      module_path: "v1_compiler.memory_governor",
      decl_name: "whole_corpus_compile_admission",
      field: WholeDeclaration
    },
    DeclarationRef {
      module_path: "v1_compiler.memory_governor",
      decl_name: "whole_corpus_compile_refusal_diagnostic",
      field: WholeDeclaration
    },
    DeclarationRef {
      module_path: "v1_compiler.memory_governor.tests",
      decl_name: "whole_corpus_compile_refuses_the_budget_that_was_sigkilled_and_admits_the_ci_runner",
      field: WholeDeclaration
    },
    DeclarationRef {
      module_path: "v1_compiler.memory_governor.tests",
      decl_name: "whole_corpus_compile_admission_is_tight_at_the_measured_demand",
      field: WholeDeclaration
    },
    DeclarationRef {
      module_path: "v1_compiler.memory_governor.tests",
      decl_name: "whole_corpus_compile_unreadable_budget_refuses_rather_than_widening",
      field: WholeDeclaration
    }
  ],
  reason: "WHY RUST IS STILL NEEDED, and it is the bootstrap constraint rather than seed-shrink debt: this decides whether resolving the corpus may BEGIN, so it cannot be a value obtained by resolving the corpus. whole_corpus_compile_seed_mirror_note argues that at length and binds this growth to the dissolution the sibling mirrors already specify rather than authoring a fourth.\n\nWHY IT IS ADMITTED AGAINST THE v1 FREEZE: gunbc.v1_maintenance_standing v1_seed_standing admits a change by PURPOSE — it serves the v2 self-host program. The instrument this refusal guards, gunbc compile --target dag over both source roots, IS the self-emission instrument, and it was OOM-killed into a silent exit-137 zero. This is defect repair on the self-host path, not growth of the seed for its own sake.\n\nHAND-ITEM DELTA: +7, all citable above, enumerated rather than counted from what was touched — the method stage0_rust_observation_seed_growth_justification records having got wrong twice. Four are production (the demand constant, the admission coproduct, the fold over it, the refusal renderer) and three are the tests that discriminate it. HAND-LOC DELTA: +173 in memory_governor.rs and +29 in main.rs against origin/main, a large share of it the doc comments carrying the receipts and the refusal strings.\n\nNO NEW UNCITABLE ITEM, DELIBERATELY. whole_corpus_compile_refusal_diagnostic is a free function and not an inherent method on WholeCorpusCompileAdmission, because DeclField offers WholeDeclaration or NamedField and neither names a method on an impl block. The sibling receipt carries one such item in prose only because its Display realization had no citable form; this one had a citable form available and takes it rather than growing that class.\n\nWHAT IS NOT GROWTH AND IS NOT LISTED: the main.rs join itself. It adds no declaration — it is an arm inside the existing Compile match, so its disposition is ExistingSeedItemModified, and listing it would net a modification into an addition census.",
  owning_dissolution_lane: "v1-hand-queue-drain" as RoadmapNodeId,
  trigger: "Delete all seven declarations when the emit path bakes gunbc.whole_corpus_compile_admission into the seed and main.rs reads the modeled admission instead of composing it — the same trigger gunbc.typed_module_cache_capacity typed_module_cache_capacity_seed_mirror_note and gunbc.host_budget_source host_budget_source_seed_mirror_note carry for their own mirrors, joined rather than restated.",
  current_boundary: "gunbc.whole_corpus_compile_admission whole_corpus_compile_admission -> v1_compiler.memory_governor whole_corpus_compile_admission -> v1_compiler.main Commands::Compile"
}
